1
00:00:02,051 --> 00:00:03,781
What if one engine could shrink the

2
00:00:03,801 --> 00:00:05,131
solar system?

3
00:00:05,151 --> 00:00:05,964
In The Expanse,

4
00:00:05,984 --> 00:00:09,031
a single invention does exactly that.

5
00:00:09,051 --> 00:00:11,256
It's called the Epstein Drive.

6
00:00:11,276 --> 00:00:13,706
Let's explore it as a thought experiment,

7
00:00:13,726 --> 00:00:16,056
grounded in real science.

8
00:00:16,076 --> 00:00:18,088
Optimistic, but possible.

9
00:00:18,801 --> 00:00:19,806
In the story,

10
00:00:19,826 --> 00:00:23,156
Solomon Epstein is an engineer on Mars.

11
00:00:23,176 --> 00:00:24,381
In his spare time,

12
00:00:24,401 --> 00:00:26,631
he upgrades a fusion engine and bolts

13
00:00:26,651 --> 00:00:28,831
it onto his own small yacht.

14
00:00:28,851 --> 00:00:31,656
It works far better than anyone expected.

15
00:00:31,676 --> 00:00:33,331
He doesn't survive the ride,

16
00:00:33,351 --> 00:00:35,006
but his design does,

17
00:00:35,026 --> 00:00:37,406
and soon ships everywhere are flying with

18
00:00:37,426 --> 00:00:37,780
it.

19
00:00:38,477 --> 00:00:39,932
So how does it work?

20
00:00:39,952 --> 00:00:42,107
At its heart is a fusion reactor.

21
00:00:42,127 --> 00:00:44,057
Tiny bits of fuel are squeezed until

22
00:00:44,077 --> 00:00:45,507
their atoms fuse,

23
00:00:45,527 --> 00:00:48,357
releasing a blast of superhot plasma.

24
00:00:48,377 --> 00:00:50,482
No metal nozzle could touch that,

25
00:00:50,502 --> 00:00:52,182
so magnetic fields shape it into a

26
00:00:52,202 --> 00:00:54,632
jet, shooting out the back at thousands

27
00:00:54,652 --> 00:00:56,682
of kilometers per second.

28
00:00:56,702 --> 00:00:59,507
Real teams are building early cousins:

29
00:00:59,527 --> 00:01:01,857
the Direct Fusion Drive at Princeton,

30
00:01:01,877 --> 00:01:03,947
Pulsar Fusion's Sunbird,

31
00:01:03,967 --> 00:01:06,072
and the magnetic nozzle of the VASIMR

32
00:01:06,092 --> 00:01:06,797
engine.

33
00:01:07,459 --> 00:01:09,414
The hardest part isn't thrust.

34
00:01:09,434 --> 00:01:10,439
It's heat.

35
00:01:10,459 --> 00:01:12,389
Pushing a big ship at 1 g takes

36
00:01:12,409 --> 00:01:13,849
trillions of watts,

37
00:01:13,869 --> 00:01:15,474
and even a tiny leak could melt

38
00:01:15,494 --> 00:01:16,124
it.

39
00:01:16,144 --> 00:01:17,549
The smart trick is to let the

40
00:01:17,569 --> 00:01:19,624
exhaust carry the heat away.

41
00:01:19,644 --> 00:01:22,099
Fuels like helium-3 release most of their

42
00:01:22,119 --> 00:01:24,199
energy as charged particles,

43
00:01:24,219 --> 00:01:26,824
which magnets steer straight out the back.

44
00:01:26,844 --> 00:01:29,479
What's left goes to glowing radiators,

45
00:01:29,499 --> 00:01:32,279
or even sprays of liquid metal droplets

46
00:01:32,299 --> 00:01:33,954
that cool off in space and get

47
00:01:33,974 --> 00:01:34,830
caught again.

48
00:01:35,501 --> 00:01:36,731
Now the fun part:

49
00:01:36,751 --> 00:01:38,181
flip and burn.

50
00:01:38,201 --> 00:01:39,493
Speed up for the first half of

51
00:01:39,513 --> 00:01:40,721
the trip, flip around,

52
00:01:40,741 --> 00:01:42,646
and brake for the second half.

53
00:01:42,666 --> 00:01:44,796
Travel time is two times the square

54
00:01:44,816 --> 00:01:47,246
root of distance over acceleration.

55
00:01:47,266 --> 00:01:50,171
Today, Mars takes about seven months.

56
00:01:50,191 --> 00:01:52,041
At 1 g, it takes two to five

57
00:01:52,061 --> 00:01:52,941
days.

58
00:01:52,961 --> 00:01:55,191
At 1.5 g, about the most people can

59
00:01:55,211 --> 00:01:56,346
handle for days,

60
00:01:56,366 --> 00:01:57,696
it's under four.

61
00:01:57,716 --> 00:01:59,856
Even Saturn is about a week away,

62
00:01:59,876 --> 00:02:00,956
and the whole time,

63
00:02:00,976 --> 00:02:02,726
thrust feels like gravity.

64
00:02:03,425 --> 00:02:04,565
In the books and the show,

65
00:02:04,585 --> 00:02:06,765
people settle the Moon and Mars first,

66
00:02:06,785 --> 00:02:07,865
slowly.

67
00:02:07,885 --> 00:02:09,440
Then the drive arrives,

68
00:02:09,460 --> 00:02:11,065
more than a century before the main

69
00:02:11,085 --> 00:02:13,965
story, and humanity rushes outward:

70
00:02:13,985 --> 00:02:16,265
to Ceres and the asteroid Belt,

71
00:02:16,285 --> 00:02:18,940
then the moons of Jupiter and Saturn.

72
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:21,665
Faster travel reshapes politics.

73
00:02:21,685 --> 00:02:24,135
Earth, Mars, and the Belters become three

74
00:02:24,155 --> 00:02:26,370
powers, trading, competing,

75
00:02:26,390 --> 00:02:28,627
and arguing over who's in charge.

76
00:02:29,362 --> 00:02:30,742
Now our twist.

77
00:02:30,762 --> 00:02:33,097
What if Mars invents the drive first,

78
00:02:33,117 --> 00:02:34,622
around 2090?

79
00:02:34,642 --> 00:02:37,817
Martians want fast travel more than anyone.

80
00:02:37,902 --> 00:02:40,182
But Mars can't build a migration fleet

81
00:02:40,202 --> 00:02:41,252
alone.

82
00:02:41,322 --> 00:02:43,502
So it licenses the design to Earth's

83
00:02:43,522 --> 00:02:46,652
shipyards, and trades it for early self-rule.

84
00:02:46,672 --> 00:02:48,202
Earth builds ships,

85
00:02:48,222 --> 00:02:49,977
Mars gets its independence,

86
00:02:49,997 --> 00:02:51,902
and the Belt opens about a century

87
00:02:51,922 --> 00:02:52,612
early.

88
00:02:53,306 --> 00:02:55,786
To compare, we built a simple population

89
00:02:55,806 --> 00:02:56,736
model.

90
00:02:56,756 --> 00:02:57,561
It's a model,

91
00:02:57,581 --> 00:02:59,036
not a forecast:

92
00:02:59,056 --> 00:03:01,011
birth rates, room to live,

93
00:03:01,031 --> 00:03:03,411
and how many people ships can carry.

94
00:03:03,431 --> 00:03:04,586
Without the drive,

95
00:03:04,606 --> 00:03:06,661
only a few million people live off

96
00:03:06,681 --> 00:03:08,811
Earth by 2200.

97
00:03:08,831 --> 00:03:11,086
With a Martian drive in 2090,

98
00:03:11,106 --> 00:03:13,021
it's over a hundred million,

99
00:03:13,041 --> 00:03:15,171
and the Belt passes one million people

100
00:03:15,191 --> 00:03:17,468
by about 2145.

101
00:03:18,154 --> 00:03:20,144
With AI speeding up research,

102
00:03:20,164 --> 00:03:22,844
this timeline might even be conservative.

103
00:03:22,864 --> 00:03:25,769
Designs, simulations, and new materials could all

104
00:03:25,789 --> 00:03:27,364
arrive faster.

105
00:03:27,439 --> 00:03:29,869
But some things AI can't rush:

106
00:03:29,889 --> 00:03:31,614
building fleets, growing food,

107
00:03:31,634 --> 00:03:33,914
and raising generations of kids in low

108
00:03:33,934 --> 00:03:34,989
gravity.

109
00:03:35,009 --> 00:03:37,989
Those take real time and real people.

110
00:03:38,009 --> 00:03:39,589
So, optimistic?

111
00:03:39,609 --> 00:03:40,639
Definitely.

112
00:03:40,659 --> 00:03:41,514
Possible?

113
00:03:41,534 --> 00:03:42,860
Let's go find out.

